This page describes a project pertaining to [[physical learning systems]], [[artificial intelligence]], [[biocomputing]] and [[intelligence]].

[[!toc levels=5]]

# Summary

This project proposes a thermodynamic physical learning system in which computation and adaptation emerge directly from the physics of self-organizing analog matter. Using techniques such as nanowire/nanoparticle networks, memristive/ionic media, and viscoelastic conductors, we treat the substrate as a high-dimensional dynamical system that learns via dynamics using fast energy-minimizing relaxation and reward-gated plasticity.

Modern AI scales with the number of adaptive degrees of freedom. The human brain sets today's benchmark with ~8x10^10 neurons and ~10^14 synapses, operating on millisecond timescales with synaptic plasticity spanning milliseconds to minutes. A particle-based analog substrate can, in principle, dwarf this capacity: a dense liquid material of ~100 nm nano-objects (although smaller and larger objects could be used instead) easily reaches 10^10 conductive particles per cm^2; because each particle potentially forms multiple junctions, different (initially random) electrical circuits can form via inter-particle contacts. These systems can trivially scale to million- to billion-fold more adaptive micro-interactions than a neurobiological system of similar footprint. These physical junctions respond at native material timescales: electronic relaxation in picoseconds to nanoseconds, capacitive/RC dynamics in nanoseconds to microseconds, and controlled ionic/structural plasticity in microseconds to milliseconds, thus enabling inference and learning bursts on microsecond or faster horizons. This combination of astronomical state dimensionality plus ultrafast local dynamics provides a new scaling frontier where compute can grow outside the semiconductor supply chain. These substrates compute by relaxing system energy, and if harnessed with hardware-in-the-loop training (black-box RL/evolution, reward-gated consolidation), such substrates could deliver orders-of-magnitude higher model capacity per cm^2, millisecond-to-microsecond training episodes, and exceptional energy efficiency, opening a path to compact, self-organizing learners that outscale digital architectures in both density and latency. Well, it may be able to do that, but only after the following feasibility assessments are completed...

# another summary

The objective of this project is to develop a **three-dimensional physical material that can be trained to perform machine-learning computations through changes occurring within the material itself**. Rather than representing a neural network explicitly in silicon or software, the material would exploit a large disordered network of nonlinear, history-dependent electrical junctions whose conductances, relaxation times, and internal states evolve in response to electrical stimulation and eventually to training or reward signals. Silver-based atomic-switch and diffusive-memristor systems are particularly relevant because Ag/Ag₂S and related Ag-in-insulator systems exhibit volatile switching, persistent switching, short-term memory, and transitions toward longer-lived states; nanoparticle and nanowire networks have separately demonstrated reservoir-computing, temporal-memory, classification, and sequence-processing tasks. The stronger goal of this project is to move beyond using such a material merely as a fixed reservoir with a digitally trained readout and determine whether the physical network itself can acquire useful internal organization through local, physically realizable training mechanisms.

One proposed baseline material is a **3D PVA/water hydrogel containing Ag/Ag₂S nanoparticles, commodity polymer or silica microspheres, and a controlled aqueous electrolyte**, with glycerol optionally added to modify solvent dynamics. PVA is attractive as the structural chassis because it is inexpensive, widely available, water-compatible, electrically insulating, capable of incorporating particles and nanoparticles, and highly tunable through polymer concentration, molecular weight, hydrolysis state, and freeze/thaw processing. The Ag/Ag₂S particles provide the primary nonlinear and adaptive electrical junctions, while micron-scale polystyrene, silica, glass, agarose, or functionalized molecular-biology beads provide an independent means of controlling three-dimensional packing, excluded volume, local particle concentration, pore structure, and chemical microenvironments. A subset of beads could carry proteins, peptides, enzymes, or nucleic acids, allowing the mechanical scaffold, active electrical network, mesoscale geometry, and molecular surface chemistry to be varied semi-independently. Agarose would be maintained as a parallel, biologically gentle host for experiments in which preservation of fragile biomolecular activity is more important than the larger mechanical design space offered by PVA.

The resulting system has a deliberately large experimental design space. Physical variables include Ag/Ag₂S concentration relative to percolation, particle size and shell properties, PVA concentration and crosslinking history, microsphere diameter and loading, mixtures of bead sizes, glycerol content, temperature, water activity, and electrical conductivity. Chemical variables include pH, ionic strength, electrolyte identity, and buffer identity; MOPS is a useful near-neutral starting buffer because it interacts comparatively weakly with Ag(I), whereas Tris, chloride, phosphate, Ca²⁺ and related species should be regarded as active experimental perturbations because they can alter Ag⁺ complexation, precipitation, aggregation, or particle surface charge. Silver surface chemistry can be varied using citrate, PVP, proteins, peptides, or oligonucleotides; citrate- and PVP-coated Ag particles, for example, respond quite differently to pH and ionic-strength changes. Broader screens could test BSA, gelatin, casein, lysozyme, simple peptides, and functionalized beads before moving to directed selection. Silver-binding peptides can be isolated by phage display, providing a route to evolve surface modifiers for a desired *material phenotype* rather than simply maximum Ag affinity. Aptamers and designed DNA provide another large sequence space because Ag⁺ can participate in highly specific metal-mediated nucleic-acid interactions such as C–Ag–C base pairs, potentially allowing nucleic acids to control local Ag⁺ availability, particle association, or junction kinetics.

simulation: A corresponding simulator should initially reproduce the computationally important degrees of freedom rather than every molecular detail. A tractable model would generate a three-dimensional random distribution of Ag/Ag₂S particles and microspheres, construct the electrically accessible particle network, and represent each nearby Ag junction with a small stochastic state model containing variables such as conductance, filament state, switching barrier, local ionic availability, and one or more relaxation processes. Kirchhoff equations would determine instantaneous voltages and currents throughout the network, while junction states evolve forward in time according to voltage-, temperature-, and history-dependent kinetics; microspheres would modify geometry and excluded volume, while buffer, coatings, proteins, peptides, or aptamers would initially enter as experimentally calibrated changes to switching rates, ion availability, aggregation propensity, barrier distributions, or relaxation times. This modeling strategy is consistent with prior work showing that Ag diffusion and regrouping can generate useful synaptic dynamics and with experiments demonstrating that disordered nanoparticle and nanowire networks can support temporal computation. The first purpose of the simulator would therefore not be to predict the exact chemistry of a formulation, but to ask whether plausible ranges of these physical parameters produce a material susceptible to useful ML training: delayed-memory tasks, nonlinear temporal transformations, sequence classification, delayed XOR, or similar benchmarks could be used to determine which combinations of connectivity, nonlinear switching, transient memory, persistent state, and local adaptation justify fabrication and measurement in the laboratory.

# plasticity speed

For this project, plasticity speed is potentially as important as electrical signal-propagation speed: the useful training rate is limited by how quickly the material can make a reliable, task-relevant internal update, not merely by how fast voltages propagate through the network. If each effective plastic event requires milliseconds or seconds, large-scale training on billions or trillions of examples becomes impractically slow even if the material contains enormous numbers of junctions; by contrast, sub-microsecond plasticity would permit effective presentation rates approaching millions of training events per second, at which point a physical network could exploit its principal computational advantage—potentially allowing millions, billions, or trillions of local junctions to update simultaneously during each event rather than having a digital processor explicitly calculate every weight update. The important materials objective is therefore not simply fast switching, but fast, repeatable, low-energy plasticity with an appropriate separation of timescales between transient electrical activity, short-lived eligibility or short-term memory, and persistent structural modification. Achieving reliable plastic changes on approximately microsecond or sub-microsecond timescales could make training on genuinely large datasets plausible and would turn massive physical parallelism into a meaningful training-speed advantage rather than merely a large number of latent internal degrees of freedom.

One proposed high-speed training architecture could treat the physical-learning system as a streaming mixed-signal accelerator in which a server feeds compact token IDs from a cleaned Common Crawl-derived corpus such as FineWeb, containing roughly 15 trillion tokens, into a local FPGA or ASIC positioned immediately adjacent to the petri-dish material; the local electronics convert each token into synchronized electrical stimulation patterns, generate teacher-forcing or reward/clamp signals, digitize or reduce the material’s outputs, and close the learning loop locally so that PCIe or CPU latency never appears in the per-token path. The host therefore streams only compact token data and receives summary statistics, while the high-bandwidth expansion into tens or hundreds of electrode channels occurs at the training head. A practical target is approximately 10–100 million learned token events per second, corresponding to roughly 100–10 ns per training event: at 10 million tokens/s, a 15-trillion-token corpus requires about 17 days for one pass, while at 100 million tokens/s it requires about 1.7 days. This performance range is fast enough to make trillion-token-scale training plausible while remaining within a regime where modern FPGA/ASIC logic, high-speed DAC/ADC hardware, local buffering, and carefully designed electrode interfaces could conceivably operate; pushing the material much faster than a few to tens of nanoseconds would likely provide diminishing benefit because electrode capacitance, RC settling, synchronization, signal integrity, sensing bandwidth, power dissipation, and closed-loop control latency would begin to dominate. The resulting materials target is therefore not arbitrarily fast switching, but reliable local plasticity on roughly the few-nanosecond to ~100-ns scale, fast enough that the material is not the bottleneck yet slow enough to remain matched to the realistic bandwidth of an integrated 64–256-channel training interface.

The available materials literature suggests that a 10–100 ns local plasticity regime is physically plausible, although it strongly favors mechanisms in which only atoms, ions, polarization domains, or nanoscale phase boundaries move rather than entire particles or macromolecular structures. Silver-based atomic-switch and electrochemical-metallization devices are particularly encouraging: optimized Ag/Ag₂S or related Ag-in-insulator junctions have demonstrated switching on approximately 1–10 ns timescales, showing that field-driven motion of a small number of Ag ions across nanometer-scale gaps can be fast enough for the proposed training regime, even though published disordered Ag/Ag₂S nanoparticle-network plasticity experiments have so far operated much more slowly and have not demonstrated comparable network-level learning speeds. Other credible mechanisms include HfO₂/HZO ferroelectric devices, where sub-nanosecond polarization switching has been demonstrated and multilevel nonvolatile conductance states can provide analog plasticity; phase-change materials, which can produce persistent conductance changes with pulses from a few to tens of nanoseconds; and VO₂ or related Mott-transition devices, which can switch on picosecond-to-nanosecond timescales but are more naturally suited to fast volatile dynamics or eligibility traces than to long-term memory. By contrast, an Arbortron based on literal translation or rearrangement of nanoparticles, microspheres, polymers, proteins, or other objects through a fluid is unlikely to support 10–100 ns learning because viscous mechanical motion is generally far slower, although proteins illustrate an important principle: large molecular structures can contain extremely fast internal degrees of freedom even when their large-scale conformational changes are slow. The most promising architecture is therefore a mechanically stationary three-dimensional network whose junctions contain nanometer-scale trainable variables—such as Ag filament length, ionic occupancy, ferroelectric polarization, domain-wall position, or local phase state—with high retention barriers at rest but strongly reduced switching barriers under an applied local field or thermal pulse. The key unresolved question is not whether such fast microscopic state transitions can exist—they clearly can—but whether they can be made gradual, reversible, low-energy, reliable over very large numbers of cycles, and preserved when billions of such junctions are embedded in a disordered network with realistic capacitance and impedance. This makes 10–100 ns plasticity a defensible materials target rather than an obviously unrealistic one, with Ag-based atomic-switch networks, ferroelectric junction networks, phase-change systems, and potentially hybrid structures combining ultrafast volatile dynamics with slower consolidation representing the strongest candidates for experimental investigation.


... "Biological synaptic eligibility traces have been demonstrated on roughly seconds-long timescales. In striatal dendritic spines, Yagishita et al. found dopamine could reinforce prior synaptic activity only within about 0.3–2 seconds after that activity.  In cortical synapses, He et al. found an LTP eligibility trace was about half-decayed by 5 seconds and essentially gone by 10 seconds.  Another striatal study directly detected a silent trace lasting about 2 seconds, with associated molecular markers elevated for roughly 2–4 seconds.  So the biological precedent is not nanoseconds or microseconds; it is typically ~1–10 seconds, enormously longer than the millisecond-scale neural spikes that originally created the trace.':

1. Yagishita S, Hayashi-Takagi A, Ellis-Davies GCR, Urakubo H, Ishii S, Kasai H. “A Critical Time Window for Dopamine Actions on the Structural Plasticity of Dendritic Spines.” Science (2014). DOI: 10.1126/science.1255514. 
2. He K, Huertas M, Hong SZ, Tie X, Hell JW, Shouval H, Kirkwood A. “Distinct Eligibility Traces for LTP and LTD in Cortical Synapses.” Neuron (2015). 
3. Shindou T, Shindou M, Watanabe S, Wickens JR. “A Silent Eligibility Trace Enables Dopamine-Dependent Synaptic Plasticity for Reinforcement Learning in the Mouse Striatum.” European Journal of Neuroscience (2019). DOI: 10.1111/ejn.13921. 




# ML options

Several modern machine-learning paradigms can be translated into physically realizable training schemes for a silver-nanoparticle or Arbortron-like evolving network. The basic task can be formulated autoregressively, analogous to language-model pretraining, by presenting a sequence and training the material to predict the next token; unlike a transformer trained by backpropagation, however, the internal physical junctions would change through local plasticity rather than host-computed gradients. The simplest Arbortron-style method is an energy-reward or reinforcement-learning rule in which successful network activity is reinforced by continued or stronger current, analogous to scalar-reward RL or REINFORCE, but this provides a weak learning signal for large output spaces. A stronger approach is teacher-forced physical learning: present the current token, allow the material to form an internal state or eligibility trace, then electrically present the correct next token so that local Hebbian plasticity associates the preceding state with the desired output; adding a global prediction-error or reward-prediction-error signal produces a three-factor learning rule analogous to policy-gradient, actor-critic, or neuromodulated Hebbian learning. Another option is physically clamped or contrastive learning, where the network first evolves freely and is then weakly forced toward the correct output, allowing local elements to respond to the difference between free and target-clamped states; this is closely related to contrastive Hebbian learning, equilibrium propagation, and coupled learning, which approximate gradient-based optimization using local physical dynamics rather than explicit backpropagation. A conventional pretrained model can also serve as a teacher through knowledge distillation, supplying dense target distributions, embeddings, future-token predictions, or curriculum selection instead of only a single correct symbol, thereby increasing the information delivered by each physical training event while leaving all persistent learned state inside the material. Together, these methods span a spectrum from reward-based reinforcement learning to supervised teacher forcing, contrastive gradient approximation, and distillation, while preserving the central requirement that the network's internal organization be learned through its own physical evolution rather than by digitally calculating and writing individual weights.

teacher-forced autoregressive training with output-error nudging and local physical plasticity.

**ML algorithm v1.** The proposed learning algorithm is a teacher-forced autoregressive predictor in which all persistent trainable state resides in the physical material rather than in digitally updated weights: at sequence position \(t\), the ground-truth token \(x_t\) is encoded onto the input electrodes while the material retains recurrent state generated by the preceding context \(x_{<t}\); the output electrodes are measured in a free phase to obtain physical logits \(z_t\), which are normalized to a predicted distribution \(p_t=\mathrm{softmax}(z_t/T)\); the training system then obtains the desired distribution \(q_t\), either as the one-hot ground-truth next token \(x_{t+1}\) or, preferably in a distillation regime, as the soft next-token distribution or compressed representation produced by a pretrained teacher model; an output-space error \(\delta_t=q_t-p_t\) is computed digitally but is not backpropagated through a software model; instead, \(\delta_t\) is converted into a physical perturbation applied to the output/teaching electrodes, causing the target-conditioned boundary state to propagate backward and recurrently through the material; junctions that retain local activity or eligibility from the free prediction respond to this perturbation through their intrinsic Hebbian, ionic, filamentary, or other plasticity dynamics, thereby performing distributed credit assignment without host-computed internal gradients; the teacher perturbation is then removed, the ground-truth \(x_{t+1}\) becomes the next input token, and the process repeats continuously. Modern extensions to test include soft-label knowledge distillation, label smoothing, learned or error-correcting distributed token codes, scheduled sampling, multi-token prediction, random feedback projections, auxiliary future-state prediction, adaptive teaching-strength schedules, and curriculum or importance sampling, with cross-entropy/bits-per-byte on held-out natural text providing the principal task metric.

**ML algorithm v2.** The proposed learner is a teacher-forced autoregressive physical recurrent network in which all trainable student parameters and local learning accumulators reside in the material, while external electronics perform fixed encoding and decoding and compute teaching signals: at sequence position \(t\), the observed token \(x_t\) is encoded onto the input electrodes, the incoming context \(h_t\) is retained, and an unnudged state \(s_t^-\) produces output measurements \(z_t^-\), decoded as \(p_t=\mathrm{softmax}(C^\top z_t^-/T)\) using a fixed code matrix \(C\); the target distribution \(q_t\) combines ground-truth next-token labels, optional label smoothing, and/or soft predictions from a pretrained teacher, whose contribution to the output gradient can be compressed to \(Cq_t\). With the input, incoming context, and trainable parameters held fixed during the comparison, a weak, calibrated output-gradient drive proportional to \(C(q_t-p_t)/T\) produces a nudged state \(s_t^\beta\); each junction compares an appropriate local statistic between the free and nudged phases to form \(\widehat g_{j,t}=[\psi_j(s_t^\beta)-\psi_j(s_t^-)]/\beta\), where \(\psi_j=\partial E/\partial\theta_j\) in an energy-based reference model, and implements a signed update with mean \(-\eta\widehat g_{j,t}\), either directly, through sparse stochastic transitions, or by accumulating local contributions before consolidation. Under suitable equilibrium dynamics this contrastive rule estimates the current-step loss gradient; approximate variants are evaluated by their aggregate update alignment and predictive performance rather than requiring precise updates at individual junctions. Task-relevant information may be distributed weakly across many junctions, so each token need only contribute a small statistical bias to the collective learned state. Recurrent representations are trained using next-token supervision and optional auxiliary losses on future predictions or selected memory readouts matched to compressed teacher representations of the observed prefix; multiscale context dynamics and explicitly modeled delayed-credit eligibility traces are additional components to evaluate, without assuming equivalence to full backpropagation through time. The teaching perturbation is removed before context advances through the same unnudged transition used at inference, the next observed token becomes the next input, and training continues. Performance is measured using held-out unnudged cross-entropy or bits per byte with learning disabled, independently initialized contexts, and controlled temporal-memory tasks that distinguish learned context processing from transient teaching effects.


**Teaching-voltage calculation.** Let the free-phase output-electrode measurements be \(z^-=[z_1,\ldots,z_N]\), after gain/offset calibration and optional normalization; these values can either serve directly as logits or be decoded through a fixed code matrix \(C\), giving vocabulary logits \(\ell=C^\top z^-\) and probabilities \(p=\mathrm{softmax}(\ell/T)\). For a one-hot target, \(q=\mathrm{onehot}(x_{t+1})\); for knowledge distillation, \(q\) is instead the teacher model's temperature-scaled soft distribution, optionally truncated to top-\(k\) classes or projected into a lower-dimensional code to reduce I/O bandwidth. The class-space teaching error is \(\delta=q-p\); with one electrode per class this can be converted directly to a current or voltage perturbation, \(I_i^{\mathrm{teach}}=I_0\,\mathrm{clip}(\delta_i)\) or \(V_i^+=V_i^-+\beta V_{\mathrm{scale}}\mathrm{clip}(\delta_i)\), whereas with distributed token codes the physical error becomes \(\delta_{\mathrm{elec}}=C\delta\) and the corresponding electrode drive is generated from that vector. The gain \(\beta\), pulse duration, current limit, polarity, common-mode bias, and clipping threshold become physical analogues of learning rate, gradient clipping, optimizer gain, and regularization; a hard-clamp baseline simply drives the output toward a predetermined codeword \(V^*(x_{t+1})\), while the preferred weak-nudge implementation applies only \(V^+-V^- \propto q-p\), so nearly correct predictions receive small perturbations and strongly incorrect predictions receive larger ones. Differential electrode pairs, zero-sum/current-balanced codes, calibrated DAC transfer functions, and closed-loop measurement of the actual electrode potentials should be used so that the intended error vector rather than parasitic impedance determines the physical teaching signal.

**Physics/ML simulator.** The simulator should represent the material as a three-dimensional disordered electrical graph whose nodes correspond to conductive nanoparticles or mesoscale conductive regions and whose edges are stochastic nonlinear junctions parameterized by geometry, capacitance, conductance, local electric field, temperature, ionic/filament state, short-term eligibility state, and longer-lived structural memory; each junction should implement experimentally calibratable kinetics such as field- and temperature-dependent Arrhenius switching, filament growth/dissolution, volatile relaxation, and STP-to-LTP consolidation, while Kirchhoff/Maxwell-equivalent network equations determine the transient voltages and currents generated by the input, output, and teaching electrodes. Simulation should proceed continuously in physical time rather than as an abstract neural-network layer sequence: encode natural-language bytes or tokens on the input electrodes, integrate the network response through the free-prediction interval, sample the output electrodes, compute \(p_t\) and the one-hot or distilled target \(q_t\), convert \(q_t-p_t\) into the prescribed teaching-electrode perturbation, continue integrating while local junction states undergo plasticity, remove the teaching signal, and advance immediately to the next token while preserving all recurrent and eligibility state. Because a literal liter-scale network could contain impossibly many microscopic junctions, development should begin with statistically faithful \(10^3\!-\!10^6\)-junction models and scalable sparse/event-driven or GPU solvers, followed by coarse-grained mesoscale networks whose parameters are fitted to smaller high-fidelity simulations and ultimately to measured hardware. The core experiments should compare no-plasticity reservoirs, ungated Hebbian plasticity, hard target clamping, error-proportional nudging, and distilled soft-target teaching on synthetic sequence tasks and then byte-level natural-text prediction, while measuring cross-entropy/bits-per-byte, effective context length, learning rate versus physical exposure, retention and catastrophic forgetting, energy per learned token, junction-update statistics, causal propagation delays, sensitivity to noise and device variation, and the scaling of performance with network volume, electrode density, plasticity timescale, and teaching-signal strength.

# Contracting projects

## Contract project: Analog electronics and physics

1) Seeking a hands-on physicist/EE/materials scientist to assess the feasibility of different thermodynamic physical learning system concepts. You'll evaluate device physics and instrumentation: junction/nonlinear conduction mechanisms, limits, bandwidth, noise/SNR, thermal noise effects, stability/drift, thermal budget, heating/cooling, and device lifetime under various stimulation or energy assumptions. Deliverables include: general feasibility analysis, brainstorming, hypothesizing, basic research/literature review, high-level sketches of simulations if feasible. Ideal backgrounds: physics, analog electronics, electrochemistry, materials science, emulsions or colloids, high-frequency signal generation/processing/ADC, high voltage electronics. This is a short-term contract or project that could turn potentially into an experimental phase. We are separately also pursuing a short-term contract for the ML side of this project.

## Contract project: Machine learning expertise

2) Looking for an [[ML|machine learning]] researcher/engineer to assess the feasibility and directions for training a black box physical learning system consisting of a high-dimensional analog substrate driven and read only at its electrodes. Deliverables include: general feasability analysis, brainstorming, hypothesizing, basic research/literature review, high-level sketches of GPU-based simulations if feasible. Ideal backgrounds: machine learning, reinforcement learning, evolutionary optimization, physical reservoir computing, energy-based/predictive-coding methods, hybrid analog-digital pipelines, and practical experience optimizing noisy non-differentiable systems.

# relevant manuscripts

Srikimkaew, O. et al. (2024). [“Volatile and Nonvolatile Dual-Mode Switching Operations in an Ag–Ag₂S Core-Shell Nanoparticle Atomic Switch Network.”](https://doi.org/10.1002/aelm.202300709) Advanced Electronic Materials 10, 2300709. Demonstrates that a self-assembled Ag/Ag₂S nanoparticle atomic-switch network can operate in either volatile or nonvolatile resistive-switching regimes, with the mode controlled by compliance current. Low-current operation (1 μA) produces volatile switching, whereas higher-current operation (100 μA) produces nonvolatile switching, with both modes remaining stable over 100 cycles. I–V analysis indicates trap-controlled space-charge-limited conduction during switching and ohmic behavior in the reset state, while the physical switching mechanism is attributed to Ag ionic diffusion and formation of conductive Ag filament pathways within the Ag₂S layer. Increasing electrode spacing requires higher compliance current to drive the volatile-to-nonvolatile transition, showing that network geometry and interconnection length directly affect memory dynamics. The work is particularly relevant because it demonstrates that the same disordered nanoparticle material can express both transient and persistent internal state depending on electrical stimulation conditions, providing a potential physical basis for simultaneously implementing neuron-like dynamics and synapse-like memory.

Srikimkaew, O. et al. (2024). [“Short-Term and Long-Term Memory Functionality of a Brain-Like Device Built from Nanoparticle Atomic Switch Networks.”](https://doi.org/10.1002/aelm.202400360) Advanced Electronic Materials 10, 2400360. Demonstrates synaptic plasticity in a self-assembled Ag–Ag₂S nanoparticle network containing more than 10³ interconnected atomic-switch interfaces. Short-term memory arises from volatile conductance changes that spontaneously relax after stimulation, while closely spaced input pulses produce enhanced conductance analogous to biological paired-pulse facilitation. Repeated stimulation progressively changes the network response and can drive a transition from short-term plasticity to long-term potentiation, directly demonstrating that stimulation history alters the persistence of the material's internal conductive state. With 100-pulse stimulation, the induced long-term potentiation persists for approximately 40 minutes before relaxing. This provides an especially important experimental precedent for physical learning materials because the material itself integrates repeated electrical events over time and converts sufficiently strong or repeated stimulation into a longer-lived state without requiring a digitally stored weight.

Wang, Z. et al. (2017). [“Memristors with Diffusive Dynamics as Synaptic Emulators for Neuromorphic Computing.”](https://doi.org/10.1038/nmat4756) Nature Materials 16, 101–108. Develops diffusive Ag-in-oxide memristors whose temporal dynamics are explicitly designed to resemble calcium dynamics in biological synapses. In-situ high-resolution transmission electron microscopy and nanoparticle-dynamics simulations show that Ag atoms redistribute under electrical bias and then spontaneously regroup under zero bias because of interfacial-energy minimization. This reversible physical redistribution generates a natural time-dependent internal state: recent stimulation changes the device's response to subsequent stimulation, while the state relaxes when stimulation stops. The devices reproduce both short- and long-term forms of synaptic plasticity, linking macroscopic electrical behavior directly to nanoscale Ag dynamics. The paper is important for the proposed physical-learning material because it establishes a mechanistic model in which ionic/atomic redistribution simultaneously provides nonlinearity, fading memory, stimulus-history dependence, and longer-lived plasticity—precisely the classes of dynamics that could serve as distributed trainable state in a material.

Mallinson, J. B. et al. (2024). [“Experimental Demonstration of Reservoir Computing with Self-Assembled Percolating Networks of Nanoparticles.”](https://doi.org/10.1002/adma.202402319) Advanced Materials 36, 2402319. Demonstrates experimentally that self-assembled percolating nanoparticle networks can function as physical reservoirs for neuromorphic computation. The networks exploit their intrinsic nonlinear, history-dependent electrical dynamics to perform benchmark tasks including chaotic time-series prediction, nonlinear transformation, and memory-capacity measurements, with performance comparing favorably with previously reported nanoelectronic reservoirs. The authors additionally demonstrate spoken-digit recognition with high accuracy, establishing that a disordered physical nanoparticle network can transform complex temporal signals into computationally useful representations. They also emulate a parallel-reservoir architecture, increasing the dimensionality and richness of measured network outputs and improving performance across tasks. This paper is especially relevant as a direct demonstration that self-assembled nanoparticle matter can perform useful temporal ML computation; however, the reservoir itself is principally exploited as a fixed dynamical substrate, making local adaptation of the material an important next step beyond the demonstrated architecture.

Zhu, R. et al. (2023). [“Online Dynamical Learning and Sequence Memory with Neuromorphic Nanowire Networks.”](https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-023-42470-5) Nature Communications 14, 6697. Demonstrates online learning using the spatiotemporal dynamics of a neuromorphic nanowire network whose nanowire–nanowire junctions undergo synapse-like resistive-memory switching. The physical network generates dynamical features that contain information about both present inputs and previous stimulation, allowing image-classification and sequence-memory tasks to be performed using the evolving network response. On MNIST handwritten-digit classification, the system achieves 93.4% overall accuracy, and the authors report a relationship between class-specific accuracy and mutual information in the physical network features. A sequence-memory experiment further shows that temporal patterns can become embedded in the network's dynamical state and subsequently recalled. This is a useful computational benchmark for the proposed system because it demonstrates that distributed resistive dynamics in a disordered nanoscale network can encode useful spatiotemporal information and support online learning, although the demonstrated learning architecture should be distinguished from a system in which all persistent trainable state is updated locally inside the material itself.

Lilak, S. et al. (2021). [“Spoken Digit Classification by In-Materio Reservoir Computing With Neuromorphic Atomic Switch Networks.”](https://doi.org/10.3389/fnano.2021.675792) Frontiers in Nanotechnology 3, 675792. Demonstrates in-materio reservoir computing using atomic-switch networks containing silver-iodide (AgI) memristive junctions embedded within highly interconnected nanowire networks. Rather than digitally simulating the reservoir, the experiment injects temporally varying spoken-digit signals into the physical network and uses its intrinsic nonlinear and history-dependent electrical dynamics to transform those signals into representations suitable for classification. The system achieves high-accuracy classification on Free Spoken Digit Dataset inputs, establishing that the intrinsic dynamics of silver-based atomic-switch networks can perform useful preprocessing and temporal computation on real-world signals. The work expands the range of silver-based materials known to support reservoir computation and is directly relevant to the proposed project because spoken-digit recognition provides a concrete benchmark for testing whether a candidate physical material possesses sufficient nonlinearity, fading memory, and dynamical dimensionality for sequence learning.

Adelnia, H. et al. (2022). [“Freeze/Thawed Polyvinyl Alcohol Hydrogels: Present, Past and Future.”](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.eurpolymj.2021.110974) European Polymer Journal 164, 110974. Reviews polyvinyl-alcohol (PVA) hydrogels formed through repeated freeze/thaw processing, in which crystallization and hydrogen bonding create a physically crosslinked three-dimensional network without requiring an externally added chemical crosslinker. The review shows that hydrogel properties can be systematically tuned through freeze/thaw cycle count, maximum and minimum temperatures, cycle duration, PVA molecular weight, and degree of hydrolysis. Additional polymers can be incorporated to create interpenetrating networks, while nanoparticles can be incorporated to create nanocomposites, providing substantial latitude for controlling mechanical, transport, and physicochemical properties. The review also covers ionically conductive PVA hydrogels, sensors, self-healing behavior, and shape-memory materials. For the proposed physical-learning system, this literature supports freeze/thaw PVA as a highly configurable host matrix in which nanoparticles, ions, biomolecules, and other functional components could be embedded while independently varying mechanical structure, water content, ionic transport, and local particle environment.

El Badawy, A. M. et al. (2010). [“Impact of Environmental Conditions (pH, Ionic Strength, and Electrolyte Type) on the Surface Charge and Aggregation of Silver Nanoparticle Suspensions.”](https://doi.org/10.1021/es902240k) Environmental Science & Technology 44, 1260–1266. Systematically examines how capping chemistry, pH, ionic strength, and electrolyte composition control the surface charge and aggregation of silver nanoparticles. The authors compare uncoated AgNPs with electrostatically stabilized citrate- and NaBH₄-coated particles, sterically stabilized PVP-coated particles, and electrosterically stabilized BPEI-coated particles. Uncoated and electrostatically stabilized particles aggregate strongly at high ionic strength and/or acidic pH, and Ca²⁺ at 10 mM induces aggregation across these particle types regardless of pH; in contrast, PVP-coated particles remain comparatively insensitive to pH, ionic strength, and electrolyte type because steric stabilization prevents aggregation. BPEI-coated particles show pH-dependent behavior. These results are directly relevant to designing a reconfigurable nanoparticle learning material because they show that particle connectivity and aggregation—and therefore potentially the geometry and conductance of a percolating network—can be strongly controlled through solution chemistry, surface functionalization, ionic strength, multivalent ions, and pH.

Babel, L. et al. (2020). [“Appropriate Buffers for Studying the Bioinorganic Chemistry of Silver(I).”](https://doi.org/10.3390/chemistry2010012) Chemistry 2, 193–202. Quantifies the interaction of Ag(I) with common biological buffers using potentiometric titration and derives stability constants and apparent binding behavior under physiologically relevant conditions. The study shows that buffers often treated as chemically inert can substantially alter the concentration and thermodynamics of free Ag⁺: HEPES interacts sufficiently strongly with Ag(I) to produce non-negligible effects, while Tris is the strongest silver-binding buffer examined and can sequester a very large fraction of available silver under representative conditions. Morpholine-based buffers are substantially weaker ligands, with MOPS being the least coordinating buffer studied; the authors recommend MOPS for Ag(I) experiments over approximately pH 6.5–7.9. For a silver-based adaptive material, this is important because the buffer is not necessarily a passive background medium: buffer identity and concentration can directly regulate Ag⁺ availability, ion transport, particle dissolution/redeposition, and potentially filament formation, making buffer chemistry an experimentally tunable parameter of the material's learning dynamics.

Zhang, X. et al. (2005). [“Biomimetic Synthesis Silver Crystallite by Peptide AYSSGAPPMPPF Immobilized on PET Film in Vitro.”](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jinorgbio.2005.05.013) Journal of Inorganic Biochemistry 99, 1692–1697. Investigates AG3 (AYSSGAPPMPPF), an inorganic-binding peptide previously identified through phage display for its specific and selective affinity for silver. The peptide was immobilized on protonated PET surfaces and used to direct biomimetic silver crystallization. Silver crystallites approximately 1–4 μm in size formed on the peptide-functionalized surface with multiple morphologies, including hexagonal, triangular, and unusual cubic structures, while XPS measurements indicated reduction of Ag⁺ to metallic Ag⁰ during the process. The study provides a concrete demonstration that short, selectable peptide sequences can modify silver nucleation, binding, reduction, and morphology. For the proposed material platform, this supports using phage display or directed peptide evolution to discover surface-binding sequences that locally alter Ag nanoparticle interactions, Ag⁺ chemistry, nucleation barriers, or filament formation, potentially providing a molecular route for tuning or spatially programming the adaptive dynamics of the material.

Ono, A. et al. (2008). [“Specific Interactions Between Silver(I) Ions and Cytosine–Cytosine Pairs in DNA Duplexes.”](https://doi.org/10.1039/B808686A) Chemical Communications, 4825–4827. Demonstrates highly specific binding of Ag(I) by cytosine–cytosine mismatch pairs in DNA duplexes. Ag⁺ binding stabilizes otherwise mismatched C–C base pairs, allowing engineered oligodeoxyribonucleotides containing C–C sites to act as selective Ag(I)-binding structures; the authors exploit this interaction to construct a DNA-based Ag(I) sensor. The result establishes a chemically specific mechanism for positioning or sequestering silver ions using programmable nucleic-acid sequences rather than nonspecific adsorption alone. In the proposed physical-learning material, analogous designed DNA sequences or evolved aptamers could potentially serve as local Ag⁺ binding, buffering, transport, or nucleation elements, allowing the ionic environment around nanoparticles or junctions to be modified through programmable molecular recognition.

Nam, J. et al. (2026). [“Overcoming Volatility in Ion Gel via Ag Doping for Nonvolatile Memristive Switching.”](https://doi.org/10.1021/acsomega.5c08106) ACS Omega 11, 5306–5314. Demonstrates that incorporating silver nanoparticles into an ion-gel memristive material can transform its memory behavior and support both digital storage and analog neuromorphic processing. The AgNP-doped device exhibits forming-free bipolar resistive switching and retains stored states for more than 10⁵ seconds even at 85 °C, behavior attributed to stabilization associated with Ag⁺-based ion pairing. The device also reproduces multiple synaptic behaviors, including long-term potentiation, long-term depression, and a stimulation-dependent transition from short-term to long-term memory. When incorporated into learning and inference experiments, the device achieves digit-recognition accuracy up to 96.4%. This paper is particularly relevant to the proposed material architecture because it demonstrates that the ionic/polymeric environment surrounding silver nanoparticles is an active determinant of memory lifetime and learning-like dynamics: changing the interaction between Ag, mobile ions, and the polymer matrix can move the same broad material class between volatile and persistent regimes and can therefore provide an experimentally accessible way to tune the timescales of physical learning.

for MNIST OCR digit recognition task:

| Paper | Task | Physical substrate | Reported result | Where learning occurs |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **Zhu et al., 2023 — “Online dynamical learning and sequence memory with neuromorphic nanowire networks”** | **MNIST handwritten digits** | Physical **Ag₂Se nanowire network**, 16-electrode device | **93.4%** with five physical readout channels | Material supplies nonlinear/history-dependent features; external digital **recursive least-squares** classifier is trained online. This is probably the cleanest experimental “OCR-like” network example for our purposes. |
| **Milano et al., 2022 — “In materia reservoir computing with a fully memristive architecture based on self-organizing nanowire networks”** | **MNIST handwritten digits** plus simpler spatiotemporal patterns | Self-organized **Ag nanowires with insulating polymer shells**, coupled to a memristive readout | ≈**90.4% MNIST** in the scaled implementation | Important qualification: the complete MNIST result used an **extended simulated NW network**, whereas physical network experiments established the underlying dynamics and smaller pattern tasks; the readout was implemented/mapped to memristive hardware. |
| **Midya et al., 2019 — “Reservoir Computing Using Diffusive Memristors”** | **MNIST handwritten digits** | Physical **Ag-doped SiOₓ diffusive memristor** reservoir + drift-memristor 1T1R readout | **83%** on 10,000 MNIST test images | Especially interesting because the reservoir is Ag-based and the **readout weights themselves are trained physically in memristors**. The reservoir itself remains untrained. |
| **Du et al., 2017 — “Reservoir computing using dynamic memristors for temporal information processing”** | **MNIST handwritten digits** | Array of physical dynamic memristors | **88.1%** experimentally with 88 reservoir devices | Physical reservoir; trained logistic-regression readout. Input images are converted into temporal pulse sequences. |
| **Ferrarese Lupi et al., 2025 — “Neuromorphic Light-Responsive Organic Matter for in Materia Reservoir Computing”** | **MNIST handwritten digits** | Commercial **PAZO azopolymer**, stimulated optically | ≈**91.4%** | Experimental responses to the 16 possible four-bit temporal sequences were measured and then used to construct MNIST reservoir representations; a software 196×10 ANN readout was trained. So it is physical-material RC, but not 70,000 complete images individually passed through the material. |
| **Lilak et al., 2021 — “Spoken Digit Classification by In-Materio Reservoir Computing With Neuromorphic Atomic Switch Networks”** | **Spoken digits, FSDD** | Physical **Ag nanowire atomic-switch network with AgI junctions** | High spoken-digit accuracy; later thesis description reports about **95%** | MFCC audio features are physically transformed by the AgI network; an external linear-regression readout is fitted. The original paper also notes that only 12 unique input streams were used in that experiment, so it is a smaller benchmark than the later Mallinson study. |
| **Usami et al., 2021 — “In-Materio Reservoir Computing in a Sulfonated Polyaniline Network”** | **Spoken digits, FSDD** | Physical **sulfonated-polyaniline organic electrochemical random network** | Up to about **70%**; all-speaker confusion matrix ≈**60%** | Physical electrochemical dynamics generate the reservoir state; software ridge regression performs classification. Particularly relevant to our hydrogel idea because memory capacity changed strongly with **humidity/ionic transport**. |
| **Mallinson et al., 2024 — “Experimental Demonstration of Reservoir Computing with Self-Assembled Percolating Networks of Nanoparticles”** | **Spoken digits, FSDD and TI-46** | Physical **Sn nanoparticle percolation network** near the percolation threshold | Entire FSDD: **3.5% WER = 96.5% accuracy** in the best parallel-reservoir configuration; TI-46 as low as **0.2% WER** with standard preprocessing | Physical nanoparticle network supplies the computation; **ridge-regression output weights are trained externally**. This is probably the closest existing nanoparticle-network analogue to what we are proposing. |

Zhu gives a very clean experimental MNIST result from a disordered Ag-containing network; Lilak directly demonstrates spoken-digit processing in an Ag atomic-switch network; and Mallinson demonstrates good spoken-digit performance in a self-assembled nanoparticle network, albeit with tin rather than silver.

In essentially all of these reservoir-computing examples, the material performs the nonlinear temporal transformation and provides memory, but a separate linear/ANN/readout layer learns the labels. Zhu trains RLS weights outside the material; Mallinson fits ridge-regression weights; Lilak uses offline regression; Usami uses ridge regression. Midya uses readout weights that are physically trained memristors, but the diffusive reservoir itself is still not trained.

# Silver nanoparticle device proposal

Here is a more specific proposal, although it is not the only plausible design.

**Summary**: This project will develop an adaptive physical learning system comprising a stationary, percolating network of silver-core/silver-sulfide-shell (Ag/Ag₂S) nanoparticles contacted by 16 independently addressable microelectrodes, combining materials engineering with hardware-in-the-loop machine learning to test whether task-beneficial transient changes in distributed conductive pathways can be selectively consolidated into persistent computational improvements.

**Device fabrication**: The initial device will use nominally 40–60 nm particles deposited on an insulating substrate with Pt-based electrodes defining an approximately 30 µm active region; particle loading, shell chemistry, contact morphology, and electrode geometry will be optimized for heterogeneous, junction-dominated conduction, reproducible nonlinear dynamics, and incremental plasticity rather than maximum conductivity. Silver-ion redistribution and conductive-bridge formation/dissolution are the candidate mechanisms for transient computational memory and retained adaptive state; voltage, pulse duration, repetition, polarity, and current compliance will be calibrated to distinguish computation, exploratory modification, consolidation, and recovery regimes. Electrical and electrochemical characterization will quantify relaxation spectra, stochastic switching, signal-to-noise ratio, drift, read disturbance, retention, reversibility, thermal loading, and endurance, with hardware-enforced current, charge, and energy limits.

**Machine learning**: On the ML side, the substrate will be treated as a partially observed, stochastic, nonstationary dynamical system whose latent state is accessible only through electrode excitation and terminal measurements, not as an array of individually addressable weights. Initial reservoir-computing baselines will use regularized linear readouts trained by ridge regression or recursive least squares; subsequent substrate adaptation will optimize a low-dimensional action space of electrode assignments, waveform-basis coefficients, pulse sequences, timing, and consolidation parameters. A state-aware black-box controller will infer compact latent-state representations from stimulation–response histories and select programming actions using contextual Bayesian optimization or Thompson-sampling bandits for short-horizon interventions, progressing to uncertainty-aware model-based reinforcement learning when action history, delayed rewards, and irreversible state changes require sequential credit assignment; SPSA and evolutionary strategies will provide derivative-free optimization baselines. Ensemble recurrent state-space surrogates will support system identification, predictive uncertainty estimation, and sample-efficient candidate selection within experimentally characterized operating limits, without assuming that a fitted terminal model uniquely identifies microscopic states. Rewards will combine task-loss reduction with penalties for programming energy, persistent drift, conductance saturation, and loss of useful response diversity; explicit stimulation budgets and interleaved reference probes will constrain exploration and detect nonstationarity.

The central learning hypothesis is that a weak programming intervention producing improved task performance can predict a corresponding retained improvement when its stimulation pattern is replayed under calibrated consolidation conditions, while unsuccessful interventions predominantly relax; exact reset, polarity-based reversibility, and autonomous junction-level policy gradients will not be assumed. Definitive internal-learning experiments will freeze input encoding, feature extraction, sampling, normalization, and readout weights, isolating material adaptation from digital decoder learning.

Benchmarks will include delayed parity, nonlinear temporal prediction, and adaptation to distribution shifts, evaluated on untouched test sequences across independent devices and sessions, with frozen-network, readout-only, shuffled-reward, energy-matched random-programming, and matched digital temporal-model controls.

**Simulation**: Supporting GPU-based simulations will couple stochastic electrical-network dynamics, ionic/filamentary state evolution, thermal feedback, parasitics, and acquisition models to compare learning algorithms and quantify sensitivity to device variability and model mismatch.

**Deliverables** will include reproducible fabrication protocols, an instrumented prototype, calibrated physical and terminal-level surrogate models, a hardware-in-the-loop training pipeline, and a controlled demonstration or falsification of retained task-directed adaptation, reporting generalization, sample efficiency, forgetting, retention, training and inference latency, system energy, and lifetime.

**Outcome**: The intended outcome is a reusable hybrid temporal learner in which the material supplies nonlinear computation, distributed memory, and persistent adaptation while an external controller supplies credit assignment and stability management, requiring coordinated expertise in nanoparticle/electrochemical materials, analog instrumentation, nonlinear system identification, reinforcement learning, Bayesian optimization, and noisy black-box control.

---

hypothesis: In an Ag/Ag₂S nanoparticle network, a weak electrode-induced modification that improves a task can predict a corresponding retained improvement after carefully increased stimulation of the same programming pattern. I would use chemically prepared Ag/Ag₂S core-shell nanoparticles deposited as a dry, interconnected film on an insulating substrate. The particles remain approximately stationary. Adaptation occurs primarily through changes at their contacts. Use fixed particles with mobile interfacial silver, and not freely moving particles in a colloid.

| Component       | Proposed first implementation                                                                                      |
| --------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |
| Active material | Ag/Ag₂S core-shell nanoparticles                                                                                   |
| Particle scale  | Approximately 40–60 nm nominal diameter; characterize the actual distribution and interfacial chemistry            |
| Substrate       | Oxidized silicon or equivalent insulating surface                                                                  |
| Contacts        | Sixteen Pt-based microelectrodes                                                                                   |
| Geometry        | Approximately 30 µm central active region, with neighboring contact gaps around 4–5 µm                             |
| Network         | Deposited, stationary particulate film; loading selected for reproducible, junction-dominated conduction           |
| Environment     | Controlled temperature and humidity; no liquid bath or intentionally mobile bulk particles                         |
| Electronics     | Programmable voltage waveforms, fast current compliance, simultaneous terminal acquisition, and a small controller |

hypothesis: Ag/Ag₂S networks can be organized into recurrent modules whose task-relevant state and retained plasticity remain controllable as the architecture grows, allowing token-level prediction errors to train increasingly expressive physical sequence representations.


