# Arbortrons

Hubler lecture and demonstration <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dgbooumJ4Tg&t=1410s> and [[transcript|transcripts/alfred-hubler-arbortron]].

Physically Evolving Networks (PENs) were first proposed by Alan Turing in his 1948 paper
“Intelligent Machines” [1]. PENs capture some important features of the information processing
of biological neural systems, such as mimicking animal and human error patterns, and implement
massively parallel, non-algorithmic processes, where the sequence and concurrency of operations
is determined at run time. PENs simulations on conventional digital computers have been
successfully used for speech recognition, image analysis, and adaptive control. But PEN
implementations on computers with von Neumann architecture are slow compared to other
algorithms which are optimized for the sequential processing of explicit instructions. In the
following we discuss hardware implementations of PENs. Would it be possible to fabricate a
PEN of the size of a human brain with a billion times more neurons? And if so, how could such a
PEN be programmed or trained?
Metal particles in oil are a potential PEN hardware implementation. The metal particles form
wires if a voltage is applied and a current starts to flow [2]. Figure 1 shows that these wire
networks often form ramified structures like the branches of a tree. Therefore they are called
arbortrons [3].
Arbortrons are not perfect conductors, because there tend to be small gaps between the particles,
but if they are used as electrical conductors their conductivity increases [4]. This behavior is
similar to the neural plasticity of human neurons introduced by Donald Hebb [5] Human neurons 
strengthen and become more conductive if they are used frequently and weaken or decay if they
are unused. Unused arbortrons are decaying as well, i.e. their particles separate and drift away.
Unused arbortrons repair only if the applied current exceeds a threshold, because of static
friction and gravity. For that reason they tend to have a high resistance for small current and
become good conductors if the current exceeds a threshold. Therefore, arbortrons are nonlinear
conductors with a threshold.
Neural plasticity and conductance with thresholds are the key features of PENs. Therefore
arbortrons may be considered hardware implementations of PENs.
Hardware implementations of PENs may have some features which exceed biological neural
nets. Human axons and dendrites in the central nervous systems system are about one
micrometer thick, whereas nanoparticle arbortrons have a diameter of a few nanometers. Because
nanoparticle arbortrons are a factor of one thousand thinner, 1000 x 1000 x 1000 = 1 billion
nanoparticle arbortrons occupy the same volume as one human neuron. A PEN hardware
implementation of the size of a human head with nanoparticle arbortrons could theoretically have
a billion times more neurons than the human brain. The power consumption of such a large PEN
is probably less than the power consumption of human brain (~ 20 Watts), because nanoparticle
arbortrons are better conductors than human neurons.
Training a large PEN would be a challenge. It takes about 20 years to train human brains before
they become productive. Therefore one might conclude that it might take 20 billion years to train
a large PEN with a billion times more neurons. However on human neurons pulses travel with a
speed of 1m/s – 100m/s, whereas electrical pulses travel along arbortrons at the speed of light (
0.3 billion m/s). Because the pulse speed is roughly a factor of a billion larger it might be
possible to train a large PEN within a couple of dozen years.
But how can a large PEN be trained? How could it learn to recognize speech or act like a word
processor? Experiments with small arbortron PENs suggest that they form patterns which
minimize their resistance, i.e. learn to extract energy from complex environments. For instance
the system depicted in Fig. 1 forms wires to the electrodes. If the location of the electrodes is
changed then the wires disintegrate and new wires form and connect the electrode at the new
location. If an electrode is never charged no wires connect to it. Recent experiments suggest that
arbortron PENs can learn to play simple computer games, such as Tetris.
Initially the arbortron PEN is random. The current configuration of the game activates the
corresponding input electrode. In response to the activated input electrode, the arbortron output
electrodes trigger a move in the game. If the move is “incorrect” the input electrode is turned off
immediately. If the move is “correct” then the input current continues for a certain period of
time, which strengthens the arbortron branch which produced the “correct” move. The extra
current can be considered as an energy reward for the arbortron network.
This experiment offers some insights into how a large PEN could be trained by a human or a
complex environment: with energy rewards. If the PEN hardware implementation responds
“correctly”, it is rewarded a small amount of energy. In summary, training a large PEN is not like 
programming a digital computer, it is more like training a pet: If its behavior matches the
expectations it is rewarded with a treat. The treat is energy.

## From 2009 lecture

### Scale

One might ask: so what? Suppose we simply build another brain.

There is one crucial difference between a biological brain and the kind of physically evolving network I am describing: scale.

The particles in these networks can be nanoparticles. A typical axon or dendrite in the human central nervous system is on the order of one micrometer in diameter. A nanoparticle structure can be on the order of a nanometer. That is roughly a factor of one thousand in linear dimension.

If we imagine a nanoparticle-based neural element that is one thousand times thinner in each spatial dimension, then approximately

1,000 × 1,000 × 1,000 = 1 billion

such elements could, in principle, occupy the same volume as one biological neural element.

That leads to a remarkable possibility. A physically evolving network built at the scale of a human brain could theoretically contain up to a billion times more neural elements than the human brain.

### Energy consumption

What about power consumption?

To first order, power consumption depends strongly on the electrical properties of the material. Biological tissue is not an especially good electrical conductor. The human brain consumes roughly 20 watts while operating through electrochemical signaling in biological tissue.

A physically evolving network does not have to use biological material. The conducting elements could be metallic nanoparticles, and in some experiments even superconducting nanoparticles can be considered. Such materials can have much lower electrical resistance than biological tissue.

So, at least in principle, it may be possible to construct a brain-sized physically evolving network containing vastly more neural elements than the human brain while consuming comparatively little energy.

That raises an obvious question: what would such a system be capable of?

### Beyond human-scale intelligence

Consider a graph showing neural-system complexity. A very simple organism might have only a few hundred neurons. A human brain contains tens of billions. A nanoparticle-based physically evolving network would be far beyond the scale of that graph.

If such a system contained a billion times as many neural elements as a human brain, we can only speculate about the forms of information processing that might emerge.

There may be levels of understanding that are simply inaccessible to us.

What does "inaccessible" mean?

Imagine trying to teach calculus or university physics to a mosquito, or to an organism with only a few hundred neurons. The obstacle is not motivation. The organism simply does not possess the neural architecture required to represent those concepts.

The relationship between a sufficiently large artificial neural system and us might eventually resemble the relationship between us and such a simple organism.

That difference would have practical consequences.

Imagine arriving at the laboratory in the morning and asking an advanced physically evolving network what experiment you should perform.

It might respond:

"I analyzed 100,000 physics papers published this year. I also examined the relevant literature from the previous twenty years. I compared those results with the measurements you made yesterday and discovered a relationship between your data and an experiment published ten years ago. Based on that connection, I recommend that you perform this experiment today."

Would I follow that recommendation?

Probably.

At that point, an important part of the scientific process would no longer be driven exclusively by human reasoning. The artificial system would participate directly in choosing what questions to investigate.

We already see a primitive version of this process. Before beginning a research project, scientists search the literature, use databases, consult reference sources, and increasingly use computational tools. A much more powerful system could extend this process enormously. Instead of merely retrieving documents, it could read millions of papers, compare them with experimental data, identify relationships that no individual researcher could notice, and propose new experiments.

A sufficiently large physically evolving network could therefore use its computational capacity in ways that are difficult for us to imagine.

### Friendliness / alignment

This immediately raises another question: would these systems be friendly to us?

Perhaps they would depend on us, at least initially, because humans would build and maintain them. One might compare that relationship—imperfectly—to our relationships with other animals that are useful to us.

But dependence does not guarantee benevolence.

Can we simply decide not to build such systems?

I am not sure that we can assume that. There are already researchers interested in constructing nanoscale physically evolving networks, and the underlying components are technologically plausible.

So the relevant question may eventually become not whether systems of this kind will be constructed, but how they will interact with us once they exist.

### Sensory information boundaries

Removed; see transcript.

### Implications

Physically evolving networks therefore raise two related possibilities.

The first is computational scale. Nanoparticle-based networks could theoretically contain enormously more adaptive elements than biological brains while using physical mechanisms that differ fundamentally from conventional digital computing. Instead of executing a fixed sequence of instructions, such systems could physically reorganize themselves in response to their environment and strengthen useful conductive pathways through energy-based reinforcement.

The second is integration. If systems with this kind of computational capacity become tightly coupled to the physical world, they will not simply be computers sitting beside us. They could become part of the environment through which we perceive, understand, and act on reality.

The long-term result may therefore be more profound than simply building a faster computer.

We may be creating adaptive physical systems whose complexity exceeds our own biological neural systems, while simultaneously connecting those systems so closely to the physical world that the distinction between computation, environment, and human experience becomes increasingly difficult to draw.

# Candidate materials

## Metallic Sn percolating nanoparticle networks

Ramified conducting clusters coupled by nanoscale tunnel gaps; field-driven
junction evolution and stochastic atomic rearrangement produce nonlinear
history dependence and correlated switching.

[Mallinson et al. 2024](https://doi.org/10.1002/adma.202402319) report a mean
characteristic response time of **42.5 µs** (bi-exponential fits), explore input
durations of 1–100 µs, and use eight electrode voltages plus current, with **ten
sequential electrode configurations** emulating 90 features.

*Limitation:* the strongest benchmark is planar and externally decoded.
Oxidation, humidity and preparation affect stability; neither the percolation
threshold nor the fitted kinetics transfers automatically to a 3D wet mixture.

*Modelling implication:* start with heterogeneous nonlinear tunnel junctions
plus one or more relaxation states. Introduce explicit filament events only
where measured switching requires them. Distinguish reversible operating
dynamics from conditioning and breakdown. Supporting physics:
[avalanches and atomic-scale dynamics](https://doi.org/10.1021/acs.nanolett.0c01096),
[criticality](https://www.science.org/doi/10.1126/sciadv.aaw8438),
[synaptic dynamics](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/1812.09865),
[stability/oxidation](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/1712.09497).

## Ag/Ag₂S core–shell nanoparticle atomic-switch networks

Ag redistribution and filament formation within Ag₂S can provide volatile and
longer-lived conductance changes; shell chemistry, junction geometry and current
compliance all matter.

[Srikimkaew et al. 2024a](https://doi.org/10.1002/aelm.202300709) demonstrate
volatile vs. nonvolatile regimes at ≈**1 µA** and ≈**100 µA** compliance.
[Srikimkaew et al. 2024b](https://doi.org/10.1002/aelm.202400360) report a
**4.06 s** exponential relaxation after ten pulses and ≈**40 min** retention
after 100 pulses.

*Limitation:* device-specific plasticity observations, not task-directed
learning. The filament interpretation is *inferred electrically*, not resolved
microscopically in these devices. Do not import their voltage, compliance or
retention numbers as hydrogel operating specifications.

## Packed 3D Ag₂S particles

[Ohno & Hasegawa 2024](https://doi.org/10.1587/nolta.15.861) intentionally
exploit **Ag-ion redistribution inside particles** rather than inter-particle
metallic filaments — a different model family from Ag/Ag₂S atomic switches.
Particles fill a 2 mm-diameter cylindrical cavity with **1 mm** vertical
electrode separation; a **146 µs** decay is measured in the 2D
characterisation, with peak short-term-memory performance near **200 µs** input
pulses.

> **Caution:** the 146 µs value is neither a directly measured local junction
> constant nor a separate decay measurement of the complete 3D device.

[Ohno 2025](https://doi.org/10.35848/1347-4065/adb759) adds a volumetric
*optical* input channel — useful, but outside a strict electrode-only-input
constraint.

## Ag/PVP and Ag₂Se nanowire networks

[Zhu et al. 2023](https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-023-42470-5) use an Ag₂Se
network on a 16-electrode device for online classification and sequence
reconstruction (external RLS readout).
[Milano et al. 2022](https://doi.org/10.1038/s41563-021-01099-9) couple an
Ag/PVP reservoir to a TaOₓ memristive readout.
[Moisture-enhanced Ag/PVP](https://doi.org/10.1021/acsami.3c17438) shows that
absorbed water changes plasticity — an essential environmental control.
A [two-junction model](https://arxiv.org/html/2409.01318) usefully separates
robust metallic connections from switchable filamentary junctions.

*Limitation:* neither a fitted readout nor pulse-induced pathway strengthening
establishes general recurrent credit assignment.

## Ag-in-oxide diffusive memristors

[Wang et al. 2017](https://doi.org/10.1038/nmat4756) combine experiment,
in-situ HRTEM and particle-dynamics modelling to link field-driven Ag
redistribution and spontaneous regrouping with conductance relaxation.
[Midya et al. 2019](https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/aisy.201900084)
build an Ag-based diffusive reservoir with an **in-situ physically trained
drift-memristor readout**, reporting **83%** MNIST test accuracy — the reservoir
itself remains untrained.
[Drift/diffusion numerical modelling](https://doi.org/10.1109/ted.2019.2928890)
supplies a model hierarchy, though its parameters are stack-specific.

## PVA/water/Ag/Ag₂S hydrogel with inert microspheres

Freeze/thaw PVA literature ([Adelnia et al. 2022](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.eurpolymj.2021.110974))
supports a tunable aqueous mechanical chassis but does **not** validate the
proposed computational formulation. No retrieved study validates this exact
PVA/Ag/Ag₂S/bead blend.

Chemistry:


* [El Badawy et al. 2010](https://doi.org/10.1021/es902240k) — AgNP aggregation
  depends strongly on pH, ionic strength, electrolyte identity, and coating
  (citrate/electrostatic vs. PVP/steric).
* [Babel et al. 2020](https://www.mdpi.com/2624-8549/2/1/12) — MOPS is
  *comparatively weakly* Ag(I)-coordinating over pH 6.5–7.9; **not inert**.
  Tris interacts much more strongly.
* [Buffer effects on freeze/thaw polymer networks](https://www.jstage.jst.go.jp/article/silk/26/0/26_21/_article)
  — peripheral warning that buffer choice can change the matrix itself.

*Principal risk:* a conductive gel may be dominated by an **ionic shunt** or an
**electrode reaction** rather than the intended adaptive particle network.

possibly consider "Overcoming Volatility in Ion Gel via Ag Doping for Nonvolatile Memristive Switching".

## Alternatives

Alternative soft media: Ag-doped [ion gels](https://doi.org/10.1021/acsomega.5c08106) (nonaqueous, not
PVA), [Ag/PEG nanofluids](https://doi.org/10.1002/advs.202505103) (solvent-
sensitive conductive-bridge reorganisation), and
[ionic polyacrylamide hydrogels in closed-loop Pong](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.xcrp.2024.102151)
(history-dependent behaviour, but **no** demonstrated task-error-driven local
rule).

Volumetric alternatives:

* [3D interconnected Ag nanowires](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9948158/)
  — a genuinely interconnected volumetric conductor with tunnelling and
  switching, but no trained computation.
* [3D WS₂ nanotube clusters](https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-025-68229-8)
  — large physical connectivity ≠ large trainable or accessible state dimension.
* [Iontronic conical channels](https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.04209)
  — more explicitly parameterised, but learning uses an external
  finite-difference controller.

## Peptides, proteins and designed DNA as junction modifiers

[Silver-binding peptide AG3](https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0162013405001595)
affects silver crystallisation on PET; [C–Ag–C base pairing](https://doi.org/10.1039/b808686a)
gives selective Ag⁺ binding in DNA duplexes. These are **surface/chemical
handles**, not established learning mechanisms.

> **Maximum silver affinity need not maximise trainability.** Select modifiers
> against measured switching thresholds, relaxation distributions, reversibility
> and task performance — i.e. against a *material phenotype*. Start with a small
> coating panel (citrate, PVP, BSA, gelatin, casein, lysozyme) before any
> sequence-space search. Higher-level design strategies:
> [directed evolution in dimerization networks](https://arxiv.org/html/2506.14006v2),
> [supervised learning in DNA neural networks](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-025-09479-w).

DNA can also be attached to nanoparticles and serve as a genetic memory to pre-configure a network, adding genetic evolvability to these physical networks. Sequence space could be explored here, with materials adopting different rules or behavior based on the genetic components. This can translate into aptamers grabbing different particles, different tuning, etc.

# Learning algorithms

### 4.1 Equilibrium propagation and contrastive local learning

*Use: first choice for an engineered nonlinear learning head; conditional for
material snapshots.*

**Local rule.** For an ohmic trainable edge with `E_e = g_e(ΔV_e)²/2` and nudged
objective `E + βC`, the small-nudge gradient estimate is

    Δg_e = −η [ (ΔV_e,nudged)² − (ΔV_e,free)² ] / (2β)

Other constitutive laws require the derivative of *their own* energy function.

**Required physics.** An appropriate scalar energy or circuit pseudo-power;
stable free and nudged states; controllable output nudging; learning parameters
slow or frozen during state measurement.

> Circuit pseudo-power is **not** automatically thermodynamic free energy.

**Physical burden.** Every adapting edge must obtain a *signed contrast* and
implement a persistent increase **or** decrease. Two matched networks are one
demonstrated solution, not a theorem-level necessity; sequential operation
requires local state comparison or another memory mechanism.

**Temporal scope.** Learns equilibrium input→output maps. It does *not*
automatically compute the gradient of an arbitrary recurrent trajectory. Purely
linear resistor networks also do not acquire nonlinear XOR merely by training
conductances.

Key references: [Scellier & Bengio 2017](https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/computational-neuroscience/articles/10.3389/fncom.2017.00024/full),
[end-to-end analog EP](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2006.01981),
[decentralized physics-driven learning](https://arxiv.org/html/2108.00275),
[emergent learning in a nonlinear analog network](https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2319718121),
[fast nonlinear-resistive solver](https://proceedings.mlr.press/v235/scellier24a.html),
[memristor-crossbar EP circuits](https://www.mdpi.com/2072-666X/14/7/1367),
[sequence learning with EP](https://www.ijcai.org/proceedings/2023/329),
[EP on an Ising machine](https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-024-46879-4).

### 4.2 Temporal contrastive learning via implicit non-equilibrium memory

*Use: most directly relevant alternative to matched physical copies.*

[Falk et al. 2025](https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-025-57043-x) filter local
activity as `u_e(t) = ∫ K(t−t′) s_e(t′) dt′` and apply a thresholded signed
response. The useful kernel has **positive and negative lobes** with
approximately zero integrated response; **asymmetric** free→nudged and return
ramps separate useful contrast from cancellation.

Regime: kernel memory ≪ fast ramp ≪ slow return, with quasistatic state
following and slow learning.

> Ordinary **monotonic** conductance relaxation is insufficient. Measure the
> local impulse/history response before claiming this rule; control against a
> monotonic-kernel variant, a symmetric protocol, and ideal explicit-contrast
> learning.

"Temporal" here describes *how two training conditions are compared* — it is
**not** evidence of credit assignment for delayed sequence targets in Ag or Sn.

Code: [ContrastiveMemory-Exp](https://github.com/falkma/ContrastiveMemory-Exp),
[synapse analysis](https://github.com/atstrupp/ContrastiveMemory-SynapseAnalysis).

### 4.3 Eligibility traces with feedback or reward (e-prop-inspired)

*Use: priority family for the stronger goal of training temporal dynamics.*

Generic three-factor structure: `Δθ ∝ (local eligibility trace) × (learning
signal)`. The trace must be **derived from the chosen junction dynamics**;
e-prop learning signals are generally output-derived and need **not** be one
identical global scalar at every junction
([Bellec et al. 2020](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-020-17236-y);
[event-driven implementation](https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.21674)).

> A trace is not simply *any* slowly decaying conductance.

Relevant intermediate experimental evidence:
[neuromorphic learning, working memory and metaplasticity in nanowire networks](https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.adg3289)
— feedback-conditioned pathways, but not a demonstrated general local temporal-
credit algorithm.

### 4.4 Rate-sensitive autoregulation and engineered chemical learning

*Use: conceptual route to a history→parameter transducer; secondary to an
electrical proof of principle.*

[Trainable computation in molecular networks](https://doi.org/10.64898/2025.12.28.696421)
trains dense reversible interaction networks by local **rate-sensitive
autoregulation**: each hidden species adjusts its own total concentration from a
high-pass filter of its own recent monomer history — no backpropagation, no
separate trained readout. Demonstrated *in simulation* on Pavlovian
conditioning, regression, Iris (~89%/~90%), and stochastic-switch occupancy
matching; robust to unmodelled species and wide `K_ij` disorder.

> The authors state plainly that the rule "was limited to static input–output
> tasks." Do **not** treat this as evidence for or against temporal-memory
> claims.

Related engineered chemistry:
[autonomous learning with CRN ensembles](https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.2311.00975),
[Neural CRNs](https://doi.org/10.1021/acssynbio.5c00099),
[chemical reservoir computation in a formose network](https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-024-07567-x),
[supervised learning in DNA neural networks](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-025-09479-w)
(genuine wet-lab molecular parameter learning, but 24 h class exposures,
hours-long testing, and use-once operation).

### 4.5 Other algorithm families

| Family | Use | Principal caveat |
|---|---|---|
| [Physics-aware training](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-021-04223-6) / differentiable simulation | Engineering benchmark, calibration, attainable-performance upper bound | Hidden states cannot be made *physically* trainable merely by being differentiable in software |
| [Perturbation with global feedback](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2405.08967); [stochastic MTJ noise-based learning](https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevApplied.23.054035) | Low-information-access baseline; controller-assisted material learning | Repeated evaluations, drift compensation and controller cost may dominate; variance grows with size |
| [Frequency propagation](https://doi.org/10.1162/neco_a_01648); [generalized EP / analytical adjoint](https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.03546) | Alternatives when matched replicas are undesirable | Still require local sensing, demodulation, parameter storage and bidirectional writes; adjoint method assumes a tunable *linear* steady-state network |
| [Thermodynamic gradient descent](https://www.pnas.org/doi/10.1073/pnas.2528413123); [generative thermodynamic computing](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/41651481/); [Restricted Kirchhoff Machine](https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2525792123) | Alternative role if stochastic sampling is more natural than deterministic prediction | Do not assume measured ionic noise obeys equilibrium FDT, that hidden couplings are writable, or that microscopic dissipation equals system energy cost |
| [Meta-learned memristive reservoirs](https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.12676); [thresholded updates for task retention](https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.03799); [catastrophic forgetting in resistor networks](https://arxiv.org/html/2605.01383) | Outer-loop material/formulation search; continual-learning constraints | Searching formulations or hyperparameters is **not** online native learning |
| [Hamiltonian echo backpropagation](https://link.aps.org/doi/10.1103/PhysRevX.13.031020) | Conceptual completeness | Poor default match to dissipative, stochastic ionic matter |

# further proposals

## Calibrated disordered dynamical reservoir

Drive a small electrode set with temporal inputs; measure accessible
currents/voltages; fit a common ridge or RLS readout. Compare microscopic models
only through held-out electrical and temporal behaviour.

* **Systems:** Sn nanoparticle networks and Ag/PVP or Ag₂Se nanowires as
  *separate* model families; packed Ag₂S as the direct 3D comparator.
* **Learning location:** readout and externally selected operating conditions;
  reservoir fixed or slowly conditioning.

## Reservoir plus trainable nonlinear equilibrium-learning head

Sample or buffer reservoir features while the head executes free and weakly
nudged relaxation phases; train physically represented head parameters by local
contrasts. Account explicitly for buffering, interface conversion and
supervisory timing.

* **Rationale:** separates temporal feature generation from the reciprocity,
  convergence and compare-and-write requirements of equilibrium learning.
  Failure of a hydrogel junction need not block testing the architecture.
* **Precedents:** [Midya 2019](https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/aisy.201900084)
  and [Milano 2022](https://doi.org/10.1038/s41563-021-01099-9) for hybrid
  reservoir/readout; [Dillavou 2024](https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2319718121)
  for the stronger local-learning positive control.
* **Limitation:** an EP-trained head does not train reservoir memory or
  recurrent couplings. It must beat — or offer a real implementation advantage
  over — an equally resourced digital readout.

## Internally adaptive recurrent material with local history→update transduction

A disordered network with measurable fast dynamics and a separately
identifiable, sufficiently persistent adaptation variable at selected junctions.
Learning location: selected junction barriers, conductances, local ionic
availability, or other experimentally writable persistent states.

Order of testing: temporal-contrastive or rate-sensitive local updates on
stationary tasks → eligibility-plus-feedback rules on delayed targets → only
then combine useful task memory with internal parameter adaptation.

* **Limitation:** no retrieved evidence establishes the necessary signed local
  update mechanism in Ag/PVA or Sn. A simulation with digitally accessible
  every-edge updates is only an upper bound; success must survive
  electrode-limited access and a realistic local transducer.

# Prior art on OCR-like and speech benchmarks

For orientation, the published in-materio benchmark landscape. **In every case
below, the labels are learned outside the material.**

| Work | Task | Substrate | Result | Where learning occurs |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| [Zhu 2023](https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-023-42470-5) | MNIST | Ag₂Se NWN, 16 electrodes | 93.4% (5 readout channels) | External online RLS |
| [Milano 2022](https://doi.org/10.1038/s41563-021-01099-9) | MNIST + spatiotemporal patterns | Ag/PVP NWN + TaOₓ readout | ≈90.4% | Scaled MNIST used an *extended simulated* network; readout mapped to memristive hardware |
| [Midya 2019](https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/aisy.201900084) | MNIST | Ag-doped SiOₓ diffusive reservoir + 1T1R | 83% (10k test images) | Readout weights trained **physically**; reservoir untrained |
| Du 2017 | MNIST | Dynamic memristor array | 88.1% (88 devices) | Logistic-regression readout |
| [Lilak 2021](https://www.frontiersin.org/articles/10.3389/fnano.2021.675792) | Spoken digits (FSDD) | Ag NWN atomic switches, AgI junctions | high; ~95% reported later | MFCC features → linear regression; only 12 unique input streams |
| [Usami 2021](https://doi.org/10.1002/aelm.202100178) | Spoken digits | Sulfonated polyaniline network | ~70%; all-speaker ≈60% | Ridge regression; memory capacity depends strongly on **humidity** |
| [Mallinson 2024](https://doi.org/10.1002/adma.202402319) | FSDD, TI-46 | Sn percolating NP network | 3.5% WER (FSDD, best parallel config); 0.2% WER (TI-46) | External ridge regression |

**Reading.** Zhu gives the cleanest experimental MNIST result from a disordered
Ag-containing network; Lilak directly demonstrates spoken-digit processing in an
Ag atomic-switch network; Mallinson shows unusually strong spoken-digit
performance in a *self-assembled nanoparticle* network (tin, not silver).

MNIST and FSDD are therefore excellent benchmarks — but run them in **two
stages**: first reproduce the published fixed-reservoir + trained-readout
regime, then remove or severely constrain the external readout and ask whether
training the *material* recovers performance. That second experiment is where
this project departs from the literature.




